WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:02.930
Good evening, everybody.

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:03.740
And welcome.

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:05.290
My name is Shannon
Rose McAuliffe.

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:07.420
And I'm the Manager of
the Student Arts Programs

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:08.590
here at MIT.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:10.863
I'm also joined by my
colleague Stacy DeBartolo.

00:00:10.863 --> 00:00:12.280
Do you want to
give a quick intro?

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:12.780
Sure.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.170
My name is Stacy DeBartolo.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:18.200
And I do Finance and
Operations for the Arts at MIT.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:23.140
And I'm also the Manager of
the Student Art Association.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.060
So both Stacy and
I work with a lot

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.770
of these really fantastic and
engaged members of the MIT Arts

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:30.730
programs.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.600
Stacy and I will be co-hosting
this event this evening.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.350
I'll have to be leaving
a little bit early

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:37.850
because I'm also
hosting another event.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:42.140
So Stacy and I'll be tossing the
talking stick back and forth.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.500
But in the meantime,
before we get underway,

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.480
I'd like to remind
everybody here

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.670
that this event
is being recorded.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.740
And it may be made
publicly available

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:54.590
on the Arts on the Radar website
or elsewhere in the future.

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.920
So if you don't wish to
appear in the recording,

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:58.970
please make sure that
your video and your audio

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:00.670
are muted during the event.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:04.120
And you can always feel free to
contact us at Arts on the Radar

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.940
at MIT dot edu if you
have any questions

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:09.440
or concerns about this.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.710
So moving right along, thank
you all so much for being here

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:13.310
this evening.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:16.430
We're really excited to hear
from this quite estimable panel

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.820
of students and recent alums
who have been deeply involved

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.770
in the Arts at MIT.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.440
These students are some of
the most active and engaged

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.290
members of this hustling
and bustling arts community.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:31.460
Their interests span music,
dance, visual arts, journalism,

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.230
sort of anything
you can think of.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:37.850
So this evening, we've
got Ethan Nevidomsky

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:39.050
moderating our panel.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:40.040
Ethan's a junior.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:44.000
And he's majoring in Comparative
Media Studies and Computer

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.500
Science and Engineering.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.300
He's also an arts scholar.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:50.300
And with that, I'm going
turn things over to Ethan

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.350
to get things rolling.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:53.420
So hi, everyone.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.460
It's nice to see you all.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.370
And so, we're going
to be going through.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:02.580
And obviously, thanks
for joining us.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.242
We're going to start
the evening with Julia.

00:02:06.242 --> 00:02:07.950
Each member is going
to get five minutes.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.280
And at the end, we'll
have a Q&amp;A. So feel free,

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:11.420
if you have something
that comes to you,

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.830
you can probably
drop it in the chat.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:14.997
But just remember, there
will be time for questions.

00:02:14.997 --> 00:02:17.600
So no need to interrupt
while people are presenting.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:18.830
With that, let's go to Julia.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:20.740
What do you have for us?

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:21.680
Cool.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:22.730
Well, hi, everyone.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:24.410
I'm Julia Chatterjee.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:28.430
I'm a junior studying Mechanical
Engineering or Course II.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:30.110
I'm currently in the
process of moving

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:33.590
into this windowless
room in Cambridge

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:35.870
with my old floor
mates from my dorm.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.690
So that's been fun.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.630
But I guess a little bit
about me and Arts at MIT.

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:44.090
Before I got to MIT, I
wouldn't say I was super

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:45.428
involved with the arts.

00:02:45.428 --> 00:02:47.720
Fashion was always something
that really interested me.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:52.460
But I deemed it a frivolous,
I don't know, hobby, I guess.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:55.130
And I definitely thought there'd
be zero conversation about it

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:56.450
at a place like MIT.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:59.490
And I was definitely
wrong about that.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:02.060
But anyway, I was really
lucky that I found myself

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.570
in the Freshman Art
Seminar run by Stacy

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:08.510
because I learned about so
many arts opportunities at MIT

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.040
that I had no idea existed.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.280
To name a few, The
Lending, the Slap, which

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:14.670
they are not doing this year.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.500
But you get to take
home pieces of art.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.470
And I've done it for
the past two years.

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:21.110
The ceramic studio, the
glass blowing studio.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:23.960
I got access to
arts classes over IP

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.110
for free that everyone else
had to pay like $100 for.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:29.270
So that was super cool.

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:31.130
And also through that
seminar, I learned

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:34.070
about fashion
opportunities that I've

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:36.770
gotten involved with
my freshman year

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:39.380
and have been involved
with ever since.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:42.410
So one is the Trashion Show,
which is an annual fashion

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:46.760
show put on by the Undergraduate
Association on Sustainability.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.050
And it features garments
made of upcycled materials

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:53.970
from designers all over
the Boston community.

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:56.580
And if you look up MIT
Trashion Show on YouTube,

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.430
there's a video
featuring me, but also

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:01.880
really cool designs that you
should definitely check out.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:07.460
And last winter, I guess, I
was the project lead for it.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:10.800
So I got to reach out to
a lot of cool designers.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.940
And it was super cool to see
dress made of wires and trash

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:14.480
bags.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.610
And I feel like the
event has gained

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:18.529
popularity in the past years.

00:04:18.529 --> 00:04:22.190
I believe it will have some
virtual form this winter,

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:22.730
as well.

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:24.797
So be on the lookout for that.

00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:26.630
But the other thing I
got really involved in

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:29.990
was the Infinite Magazine, which
is MIT's 90s fashion and design

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:31.582
publication.

00:04:31.582 --> 00:04:33.290
And when I first joined
my freshman year,

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:36.330
I really had no idea
what I was doing.

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:38.300
I didn't have much
graphic design experience.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.550
But I just always knew that
the magazine was something

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:42.560
that I wanted to be a part of.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.130
And every semester, I just felt
like I needed to contribute.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:49.640
So, since my freshman year, I've
knit a bra for that magazine

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.190
and made earrings out of fruit.

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:54.810
And now, I'm the Editor
in Chief for this issue.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.390
So I'm super excited about that.

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:58.840
We're making another
virtual issue.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:02.570
And I did want to
share my screen

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:05.465
to show what I made
this past issue six.

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:14.860
So what I ended up
doing is, I initially,

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.290
when we were still
on campus in January,

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:19.840
I just knew I wanted
to make pants.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:21.110
But I didn't know why.

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:24.670
And then when I got home,
because of quarantine and MIT

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:26.830
closing, I found all
these really cool fabrics

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:32.110
in my closet and decided that,
as a reaction to this one size

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:35.663
fits all, everyone leave
and everybody put on a mask

00:05:35.663 --> 00:05:37.330
situation that we
were just thrown into,

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:40.420
I made one size fits all pants.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:42.880
And I had everyone I
was quaranting with

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:47.417
or my friend who wasn't
seeing anyone model them.

00:05:47.417 --> 00:05:49.000
And so, this is my
sister's boyfriend.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.200
And then that's my friend.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:53.485
And then my dad also
got in on the fun.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:58.390
But I wanted to show this
because, as I mentioned,

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:01.780
we're making another
virtual issue this fall.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:05.860
And I found that making when
you're in a virtual world

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:09.320
is something that I
found really therapeutic.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:12.760
And I always felt like I was
really unproductive when I

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:15.320
was quarantining or isolating.

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:17.230
And so, making things
was a way to show

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:19.690
that I was doing
something with my time

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:21.760
and doing something
that I enjoyed.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:23.600
So if you're
interested in joining,

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:25.810
follow us on Instagram
at Infinite Magazine.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:28.450
And we'll be posting
about an info session

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:31.690
that we'll be hosting soon.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:32.190
Yeah.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:33.670
And I guess going
forward, at MIT

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:35.440
and getting involved
with the arts,

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:38.350
I definitely don't
want to dismiss fashion

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:39.670
as I did before I got here.

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:41.680
Because there's so much
research in fashion,

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:44.320
whether it's C-sale,
doing robotic knitting,

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.100
or Course III doing space suits.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:49.710
Yeah.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:51.010
That's all I have.

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:52.790
Thank you.

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:53.720
Great.

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:55.830
OK.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:56.790
That was really nice.

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:57.390
Thanks.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:59.760
So moving on, sorry.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:02.220
Give me a second.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.560
My stuff is also acting up.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:05.100
Yeah.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:08.730
So moving on, next we'll
be going to Chucho.

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:10.658
Are you ready to present?

00:07:10.658 --> 00:07:11.506
Yeah.

00:07:11.506 --> 00:07:14.640
OK, hey-- take it away.

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:16.203
Thank you.

00:07:16.203 --> 00:07:16.870
Hello, everyone.

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:19.350
My name is Chucho Ocampo.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.680
I'm an artist and an architect.

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:28.890
I am a second year now at
ACT in the ACT program.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:33.410
Well, I am here in
Cambridge right now.

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:39.710
And I think that the
question is quite good.

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:42.530
What is it like to be
an artist inside of MIT?

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:48.520
I think it's a huge, I
think it's a huge privilege.

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:52.420
And it's a very fun thing to be.

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:56.830
Because I think-- and maybe
I can speak in regards to ACT

00:07:56.830 --> 00:08:00.210
the students, as well--

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:02.610
we as artists tend to
play with a lot of things

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:05.760
and are very curious
about a lot of things

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.320
that happen, especially
inside the institution.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:11.610
So being here means
you get to play

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:13.290
with a lot of these things.

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:16.170
And you get to collaborate, as
well, with a lot of very, very

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:18.510
interesting people.

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:25.260
So I'll say, first of all,
that's kind of the main

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:28.680
or the most attractive
thing, being surrounded

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:33.059
by so many people that are
very willing to share ideas

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:35.610
and just collaborate.

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:39.900
And kind of lending also some
of the tools and the things

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:41.480
they are developing.

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:45.150
And it's back and forth,
which is super interesting,

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:48.950
especially being an
artist that makes stuff.

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:52.290
And it is also inside,
in between Architecture,

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:57.390
Media Lab, ACT has a very
interesting, I think,

00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:03.330
space inside of MIT, which
I think is super fun.

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:06.760
Another amazing,
amazing thing is,

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:12.385
I think, the supports and
the funding MIT offers,

00:09:12.385 --> 00:09:16.690
especially Arts at MIT
and all of the grants

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:22.120
that are available that are
super precious for production,

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:28.030
which makes prototyping and
making [AUDIO OUT] possible.

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:31.310
And that's not very
common in my experience.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:35.250
So being inside
this place and also

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:39.520
having the resources available
makes it a completely different

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:41.540
experience.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:44.570
And also programs such
as the Residency Program

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:46.750
that Arts at MIT
has also relates

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:50.950
to ACT or MISTI that also offers
certain things for artists,

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:52.400
as well.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:55.180
And yeah, I think,
overall I don't

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:56.620
have like a specific project.

00:09:56.620 --> 00:10:00.190
I think just for
the past year, I

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:06.540
have been surrounded in
this crazy environment

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:09.250
but also felt very
much supported.

00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:14.650
Not just financially but also
via this really incredible

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:19.120
array of people
and facilitators.

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:19.870
I think that's it.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:24.170
Really great.

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:28.700
And you were also working
on a collaborative project

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:30.420
with Aarti, right?

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:31.020
Yes.

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:31.520
Yes.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:33.500
I think Aarti can jump in.

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:34.000
Yeah.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:35.590
And feel free to talk together.

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:37.430
Or just let her take over.

00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:41.360
One of these things,
especially, I mean, obviously

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:42.740
when you're inside this--

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:43.640
as I was saying--

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:45.530
if you're inside,
you're being supported,

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:47.480
you're building things.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:50.670
And then COVID hit, of course,
and changed a lot of things.

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:56.030
And so, I think most of us
had to shift media or medium,

00:10:56.030 --> 00:11:04.370
and shift towards more
digital platforms or software

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:07.010
or other types of supports.

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:12.980
And we realized we were
working on very similar ideas

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:14.030
altogether.

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:16.820
So we decided to
collaborate and make

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:21.230
a project all of
our cohort together.

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:23.903
And maybe Aarti can tell you
a little bit more about that.

00:11:23.903 --> 00:11:24.570
So I don't know.

00:11:27.237 --> 00:11:28.070
You're muted, Aarti.

00:11:28.070 --> 00:11:29.290
You're muted right now.

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:29.790
Sorry.

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:30.560
Yeah.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:33.700
So I'm going to quickly
share screen and talk over

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:37.570
that so that you can have
an idea of what exactly

00:11:37.570 --> 00:11:40.490
we were doing.

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:40.990
So--

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:44.260
I'm also sharing the link
of the web page in the chats

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:47.540
so you can also go through.

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:48.400
So yeah.

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:53.600
So this is something which, as
Chucho said, the five of us--

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:55.280
we are five of us
in a cohort-- and we

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:59.240
decided that what we had a very
good rapport with each other.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:02.150
And then we decided we certainly
were in different places

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:04.422
without the kind
of support that we

00:12:04.422 --> 00:12:06.380
give each other, which
is very important for us

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:07.855
on our own projects.

00:12:07.855 --> 00:12:09.230
And we decided
that we would have

00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:12.110
to find a way to work together
regardless of location.

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:16.520
And we came up on this
idea of putting together,

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:18.740
it came off the
exquisite corpse kind

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:22.790
of general conceptual
framework, which

00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:26.900
is to be able to put together
different kinds of material

00:12:26.900 --> 00:12:29.690
and to construct something
out of different pieces

00:12:29.690 --> 00:12:31.310
of multiple things.

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:33.890
I mean, whatever it is that
whoever brings to the table.

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:35.020
So that was this.

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:39.830
So each of us sort of modeled
particular landscapes,

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:42.920
modeled particular items.

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:45.200
Maybe it was a room,
maybe it was a walk,

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:46.970
maybe it was an object.

